2026-07-28 FINANCIALS + RELATIONSHIPS BURST (still Edges started, deliberately). Costed 6 more companies to 13 of 24, and grew the edge set from 6 to 16.

THE CHEAP WIN WAS A CORRECTION, NOT A CONFIRMATION. This map's runbook hypothesised that Cameco/NexGen/Denison failed tools/xbrl_extract.py because its annual-period selection was missing an ifrs-full annual instance for 40-F filers. That was wrong. The tool dropped every fact whose unit was not USD, at line 69, BEFORE any period logic ran. Cameco tags ifrs-full in CAD: revenue CAD 3,481,933,000, attributable profit CAD 589,577,000, plus capex and R&D, all present, all annual, all discarded, and the tool answered "no annual revenue tag" -- which a reader takes to mean the filer does not disclose it. Rio Tinto had only ever worked because Rio tags in USD. Fixing it cost three coupled edits, because widening the filter alone would have made report() divide CAD by 1e9 and print it as USD: the filter now takes any ISO-4217 unit, the display labels the currency on every line and warns when the chosen figures span two currencies, and a Cameco CAD fixture guards both. Cameco and Denison then costed from XBRL for nothing. NexGen has no revenue concept in ANY currency and stays EMPTY, not 0.

The new fixtures were mutation-checked rather than trusted: five mutants (revert the display half, revert the filter half, drop the mixed-currency warning, unanchor the currency regex, drop the convert note) each made the suite go red on named cases. The first run of that harness found a REAL defect in my own test: with the filter reverted, pick() returned None and a bare p["val"] raised TypeError, so the suite died with a traceback instead of naming the failing case AND every check after it stopped running. A suite that crashes reports less than one that fails; the accessor is now defensive and that mutant reports six named DEAD cases.

Orano, Urenco, Kazatomprom and EDF were costed by two sequential workers off publisher PDFs. Route discovery was paid up front in the main loop, and the FX rate was computed once here and handed to both workers as a literal, so no renormalisation pass was needed. That rate is itself a measurement that had to be re-taken: Yahoo's monthly CADUSD series returned 13 buckets for a 12-month year, with March duplicated, October missing and January 2026 included. Averaging it would have given 0.7175 instead of the correct 0.7158. The daily series (257 closes, 12 months, cross-checked against the mean of monthly means) is what every conversion here uses.

RELATIONSHIPS. The pre-registered test worked: IFRS 12 requires naming subsidiaries, joint ventures and associates WITH ownership percentages, and Kazatomprom's notes duly printed roughly 30 entities and their splits. That is this map's equivalent of pharma's >10% customer-concentration rule, and it should be the first thing any future burst here opens. The French fuel cycle was the other rich seam: Orano's and EDF's related-party notes each enumerate the same three front-end services, giving Orano->EDF as uranium, UF6 and enrichment supply, plus the ATR reprocessing contract running to 2040. Urenco's own annual report names EDF as its largest ever contract, which closes gap-10-2.

## HONEST WEAKNESSES

- STILL NOT MAPPED, and the label stays "Edges started". 13 of 24 costed, zero timeseries rows, and 11 of 24 companies still have no edge at all. Three of the seven chokepoints (TVEL, CNNC, Honeywell/Metropolis) still carry no sourced edge; the count moved from 3 of 7 evidenced to 4 of 7.
- TWO CHOKEPOINT FLAGS MAY BE UNCLOSEABLE BY THIS ROSTER RATHER THAN BY THE WORLD. TVEL's dependants are VVER operators and this roster contains none; CNNC's dependants are its own subsidiaries. Both are filed as out-of-scope gaps naming the company that would have to be ADDED (gap-10-10, gap-10-11). That is a finding about the roster, not evidence for or against the flag.
- THE HONEYWELL EVIDENCE POINTS AGAINST ITS OWN FLAG. A candidate Honeywell->Uranium Energy edge was DROPPED: UEC's 10-K names Metropolis only as a place its uranium is stored, not as a supplier, and the same sentence names Cameco's Ontario facilities as an alternative. Storage is not supply, and a named alternative cuts against a sole-source premise. Recorded as gap-10-9. Honeywell is still flagged chokepoint=yes; that flag is now weaker than when it was set.
- EDF IS UNVERIFIED BY TOOL. The whole edf.fr host is unreachable to this repo's verifiers here: system Python links LibreSSL 2.8.3 and cannot negotiate the TLS version the host requires, and curl gets 403. EDF's figures and the EDF->Framatome edge were read by a human and are cited correctly, but verify_sources and verify_edges cannot check them (12 of 13 rows, 9 of 10 staged edges). The citations were deliberately NOT swapped for weaker reachable ones. gap-10-6, gap-10-7.
- FOUR ORANO->EDF EDGES REST ON ONE BUNDLED CLAUSE EACH SIDE. Orano's related-party note and EDF's note 22.1.2 each enumerate "uranium sales, conversion and enrichment services" in a single sentence rather than pricing or quantifying them separately. Two independent documents agree, and the map's vocabulary has a distinct term per service, so they are drawn as three supply edges plus reprocessing. A reader should know they come from two sentences, not four contracts.
- THE FRAMATOME->EDF EDGE IS EIGHT YEARS OLD, and structurally has to be. EDF's FY2025 statements never name Framatome as its fuel fabricator because Framatome is 80.5% owned and consolidated, so the intercompany fuel transaction is eliminated. The only source naming both parties is a 2018 Framatome press release covering a 2023-2032 period. gap-10-8.
- KAZATOMPROM'S CAPEX IS THE NARROW FIGURE and is arguably the wrong one for an in-situ-recovery miner. It counts purchases of PP&E and intangibles (KZT 88,859m, USD 0.1715B) to stay consistent with every other row in this atlas, and EXCLUDES mine development assets of KZT 136,164m. On the broad definition it would be USD 0.4437B. The row says so; the rendered number does not.
- URENCO'S NET INCOME CANNOT BE CROSS-CHECKED. It discloses no non-controlling-interest split anywhere, so attributable equals total by construction and the check that validated Orano, EDF and Kazatomprom is unavailable. gap-10-14.
- KAZATOMPROM'S PRIMARY STATEMENTS ARE SCANNED SIGNED IMAGES, read visually rather than from extractable text.
- TWO DEFECTS FOUND IN tools/review.py AND DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED, because it is shared by all ten maps and should not change silently inside a data burst. (1) R8 matches a gap's "A -> B" subject against an unordered {from, to} set, so a directional gap fires on the REVERSE edge too: gap-10-8 concerns Framatome->EDF fuel fabrication and also fires on EDF->Framatome ownership, a different relationship it says nothing about. This contradicts R8's own comment that it "only speaks when a gap row points at ONE named edge". (2) CAVEAT_CUE has no term matching a plain statement of evidence age, so an edge whose description carries an explicit NOTE ON EVIDENCE AGE still reads as clean to it. That is the same false-positive class the file already documents for MP Materials->Lynas, where the recorded fix was to widen the checker and never to reword the data. Both R8 findings on this map are false positives.
- tools/xbrl_extract.py still returns NOTHING AT ALL for a pre-revenue filer, not merely an empty revenue, because reporting_period() keys off revenue alone. NexGen's CAD net income and share count are reachable in its companyfacts and remain uncostable for that reason. Not fixed here: it needs its own mutant, and NexGen is not a chokepoint.

NEXT: this map's cheapest remaining wins are ROSTER ADDITIONS, not more document reading. Adding a VVER operator (MVM Paksi Atomeromu or Slovenske elektrarne) would evidence TVEL; adding China National Nuclear Power (601985.SH), China Uranium Development and SNURDC would evidence CNNC and let the map draw the Russia-to-China ownership shift in Kazakh uranium that gap-10-12 records but cannot render. After that, a timeseries pass is what separates this from Mapped.
